output.rationale:
`requirements.ci.review_criteria` states almost entirely observable reviewer behavior. That covers what the reviewer must assess, how it derives expected outcomes, what a counterexample must show, what counts as a gap, and how it treats distributed requirements, examples and ambiguity. Any alternative way of building the reviewer that gives the same assessments still satisfies those clauses, so they are not structural. I found no prescribed library, algorithm, signature, type, field or ownership obligation.

One clause is a delegation: "Structural checks follow {cite}`requirements.authoring.structural_prompt`". A second part of the text is also carried out by other declarations. That part covers the guidance for requirement and example reviews, the test-adequacy criteria, the testing exemptions and the judgment rules. Two requirements carry `{impl}` references to the subject: `requirements.authoring.structural_prompt` and `requirements.authoring.initial_prompt`. The text says to assess whether such a requirement's text carries out its part, and not to reassess its implementation or evidence. I assessed only that.

- `requirements.authoring.structural_prompt` carries out the structural-check part. It fixes a structural-only prompt. That prompt:
  - asks whether implementation roots support the stated structural obligations;
  - requires a quoted clause and a behavior-preserving alternative for each candidate obligation;
  - separates structural from behavioral obligations;
  - requires success when every clause is observable-only;
  - includes `{{judgment_rules}}`.
  It also keeps the structural and behavioral conversations apart, matching the subject's independence requirement.
- `requirements.authoring.initial_prompt` carries out the behavioral-prompt part. Its prompt:
  - assesses test adequacy only;
  - reassesses neither refining requirements' implementations nor cited requirements;
  - appends the testing criteria from `requirements.authoring.testing_bar`;
  - includes `{{judgment_rules}}`.
  Its text says its review guidance follows `requirements.ci.review_criteria`.

The prompt templates therefore carry out the clauses delegated to them. No unmet structural obligation remains and no implementation marker is missing. Test adequacy is left to the behavioral criterion.
